course: dictionary
lesson: 4193
title: Goody and its many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, so check which meaning fits the sentence. «At a party in Toronto, Leo slipped a second goody bag into his coat while nobody was looking.» A goody bag is full of nice treats, so here goody means a treat or a small present, and Leo is sneaking an extra one.
explain: Goody can also mean a good character in a story or film, the opposite of a baddie. When children play goodies and baddies, they pretend to be the heroes or the villains. Ask yourself: does the sentence fit this meaning?
explain: Goody can be a happy shout too: «Goody!» means «great!» Goody-goody is different. It describes a child who tries to look good to grown-ups in a smug way, and it can sound teasing or unkind.
fact: Goodies means nice things, especially treats such as sweets, cakes or small presents.
fact: A goody is a good character in a story or film, the opposite of a baddie.
fact: «Goody!» is a happy shout meaning «great!»
fact: «Goody-goody» is informal and can be unkind: it describes someone who looks good to grown-ups in a smug way.
fact: Goody is informal, so you will hear it in chatty speech and stories more than in very formal writing.
exercise: «At a party in Toronto, Leo slipped a second goody bag into his coat.» What are the goodies in this sentence? | A rude word for a grown-up | *Sweets and small presents | A kind of story character | Grey, wet weather
exercise: Maya shouts, «Goody!» when she wins the prize. What does she mean? | A sad sigh | Be quiet | *Great! | Goodbye, see you later
exercise: Sam says Priya is a goody-goody. What does this suggest? | She has a bag full of sweets | *She tries to look good to grown-ups in a smug way | She is a hero in a film | She is always late for school
test: In the game of goodies and baddies, what is a goody? | A sweet treat in a bag | A grey, wet day | *A character on the good side, like a hero | Someone who is always late
test: What is a goody bag? | A bag for rubbish | A bag for carrying school books | A bag for muddy hiking boots | *A bag full of nice treats and small presents
exam: In «Oh goody, we are going to the seaside!», what does goody mean? | *Great, I am pleased | Oh no, that is bad news | Treats to eat | Goody-goody, a smug child
